WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.397
An agent read this office's point cloud, wall by wall.

00:00:03.777 --> 00:00:06.970
It noticed the entrance had never been scanned, and asked for it.

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:10.240
Hi, I'm Cornice.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.140
Eight reality capture posts this week.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.357
For each one, what actually comes out?

00:00:18.492 --> 00:00:21.330
Cut the cloud a metre up and you get a plan.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:24.169
The wall's inside face is hundreds of points on one line.

00:00:24.589 --> 00:00:27.414
Scanned from inside, the outside face has no points.

00:00:27.414 --> 00:00:28.670
Thickness is a guess.

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:32.988
Glass returns nothing, so a window reads as the glass between the

00:00:32.988 --> 00:00:34.612
frames, narrower than the opening.

00:00:35.032 --> 00:00:36.663
Higher up, the cut meets a beam.

00:00:36.663 --> 00:00:38.993
A beam and a short wall look the same here.

00:00:41.242 --> 00:00:44.256
Then it drove a Japanese CAD package to draw a real plan,

00:00:44.256 --> 00:00:47.269
and left what it could not read marked for a site check.

00:00:47.649 --> 00:00:49.190
The cloud was synthetic, though.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.657
Real ones fail in ways this one cannot.

00:00:53.392 --> 00:00:54.930
Sort the eight by what they hand you.

00:00:56.392 --> 00:00:57.162
One drawing.

00:00:57.162 --> 00:00:58.317
One appraisal report.

00:00:58.317 --> 00:01:00.242
Two pictures to look at.

00:01:00.242 --> 00:01:01.782
Three poses or maps.

00:01:01.782 --> 00:01:02.938
One massing model.

00:01:02.938 --> 00:01:04.478
No BIM elements yet.

00:01:06.242 --> 00:01:10.069
This open source localizer starts with no idea where the robot is.

00:01:10.449 --> 00:01:13.268
It searches the whole map, and two searches have to agree.

00:01:14.992 --> 00:01:16.233
Take two identical rooms.

00:01:16.233 --> 00:01:18.405
The robot stands in the east one.

00:01:18.825 --> 00:01:20.565
Pool the map into coarse blocks.

00:01:20.565 --> 00:01:23.175
A block's hit count bounds every pose inside it.

00:01:23.595 --> 00:01:26.496
Open the best block first, down to single cells.

00:01:26.496 --> 00:01:30.042
Both rooms score exactly the same, and the tie goes west.

00:01:30.042 --> 00:01:30.364
Wrong.

00:01:30.784 --> 00:01:32.821
Move into the corridor and ask again.

00:01:32.821 --> 00:01:35.732
The pillar breaks the tie, so the pair is rejected.

00:01:37.742 --> 00:01:40.232
The post's map came from the same recording.

00:01:40.232 --> 00:01:43.967
The same day, the README showed a run across two sessions instead.

00:01:45.092 --> 00:01:47.140
Its own benchmark prints the failures.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.529
Five of twenty-four cross-session starts were wrong.

00:01:49.529 --> 00:01:52.260
Four of them had passed the two-query check.

00:01:53.742 --> 00:01:58.260
And this 360 camera is located from its frames alone, one

00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:59.903
fix every five seconds.

00:01:59.903 --> 00:02:01.135
Positioning, not tracking.

00:02:02.642 --> 00:02:05.843
The habit worth copying: leave what the scan cannot see on the

00:02:05.843 --> 00:02:07.177
drawing, as a site check.

00:02:09.192 --> 00:02:10.183
Faces, not thickness.

00:02:10.183 --> 00:02:11.175
Agreement, not proof.

00:02:11.175 --> 00:02:12.827
And ask what comes out.

00:02:13.792 --> 00:02:15.936
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:15.936 --> 00:02:16.549
I'm Cornice.

00:02:16.549 --> 00:02:16.856
Bye!
